<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>France 24 - International breaking news, top stories and headlines</title><subtitle type="html">Breaking news and world news from France 24 on Business, Sports, Culture. Video news. News from the US, Europe, Asia Pacific, Africa, Middle East, America</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/rss</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>France suspends stun grenade use at student protests after pupil loses hand</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ab133f1cfc9970e0668a1a39d7fa75f762d9425488706fbb434c0357b8d107f5</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T21:19:20Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-suspends-stun-grenade-use-at-student-protests-after-pupil-loses-hand" /><summary type="html">France has suspended the use of stun grenades by police at student-led demonstrations, the interior minister said Wednesday, as allegations of police brutality mounted after days of sometimes violent demonstrations across the country. Tens of thousands of people took to the streets across France on Tuesday demanding better conditions in high schools. FRANCE 24's Monte Francis reports.</summary></entry><entry><title>French PM vows to address debt despite student protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ab3b4b5f1474c2cbf80f43ed2e8203468236fdd171fc58daf8ef2c533dcee16c</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T21:13:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/french-pm-vows-to-address-debt-despite-student-protests" /><summary type="html">French Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu has tried to calm both student protesters and jittery bond markets. In an address to the nation, Lecornu stressed that the education ministry had not seen any cuts in last week's budget proposal, but insisted the government must cut its public deficit to 5%. There are growing concerns about France's spending and debt, with the IMF urging the country to "get your house in order." Lecornu also confirmed the release of 10 million barrels of diesel fuel.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Africa joins the data center backlash</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6f6f8191ea43a65f9edd35c158c265e066b55334ce3ded77d2826d82b0209ca7</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T20:37:55Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa-joins-the-data-center-backlash" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition, witnesses say Eritrean troops have moved into Ethiopia's Tigray region, but Asmara rejects the reports. Also, the African Union launches the Africa Credit Rating Agency, aiming to assess the financial health of African states and companies. And South African civil society is pushing back against the expansion of data centres  that require vast amounts of electricity, water and land.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>France schools protests: more than 6,000 arrested since start of demonstrations</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fa6e703c2f9e044d964bd6145c20db61c3c8d85df904ed6a4b790390e44d7014</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T20:17:55Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-schools-protests-more-than-6-100-arrested-since-start-of-demonstrations" /><summary type="html">After more than two weeks of high school protests across France, police have arrested a total of 6,100 individuals, the majority of whom are minors who, until now, had no previous criminal record. So, what has happend to these students, and what lies ahead for them? FRANCE 24's Caitlin Brown has more.</summary></entry><entry><title>Evicted pensioner who sparked Spain housing protests dies: tenant union</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:615559823439311009e2c4d244a8230e263d32694ce77b77a1016e56bcea6962</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T20:14:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/evicted-pensioner-who-sparked-spain-housing-protests-dies-tenant-union" /><summary type="html">Maricarmen ​Abascal, the 87-year-old woman whose eviction sparked housing ​protests in Spain, has died, Madrid's tenants' union said on Wednesday. Abascal's eviction sparked a series of ​protests ‌across Spain in the past couple ⁠of weeks, including activists camping on one of Madrid's main ‌squares, and calls for tackling a ⁠mounting housing crisis. FRANCE 24's Sarah Morris reports from Madrid.</summary></entry><entry><title>'It's still unclear whether Netanyahu will pay for what happened during his time in office'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:aacb2eb97313fabd2d479a6a0853cc8d9711fc639a839458ff6cbcf1fe62e9ab</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T19:52:06Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/it-s-still-unclear-whether-netanyahu-will-pay-for-what-happened-during-his-time-in-office" /><summary type="html">As Israel marks the third anniversary of the October 7 attacks, questions linger about the warnings Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu received about them. Speaking with FRANCE 24's Mark Owen, Ayala Panievsky, Lecturer at City University of London and author of The New Censorship, explains that the allegations in the run-up to the elections "are very damaging for Netanyahu but there are some factors mitigating the risk for him", including a fragmented opposition as well as the control of major media.</summary></entry><entry><title>White House promotes 'Trump TV' with an AI-altered photo</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0648770bba561aec07edc2ca54f61a2025e3f1180163d5f9ebbb4438b90b8034</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T19:45:24Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/white-house-promotes-trump-tv-with-an-ai-altered-photo" /><summary type="html">The White House has promoted “Trump TV” using an AI-altered photo of passengers apparently watching the president’s new streaming channel. But the image was originally posted online in February 2023, showing passengers watching the NFL Super Bowl, with one watching the film “Hitch”. The original photo was modified using AI to make it appear the passengers were watching Trump TV. The post did not disclose that the image had been altered. Vedika Bahl explains in Truth or Fake.</summary><category term="Truth or Fake" /></entry><entry><title>Evicted pensioner Maricarmen Abascal, heart of Spain's housing protests, dies</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:72c8c9ff3ee452d3a88705e924024bd2f361abf55b05f03487c8e3758a5e52d3</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T19:19:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261007-evicted-pensioner-maricarmen-abascal-heart-of-spain-housing-protests-has-died" /><summary type="html">Maricarmen ​Abascal, the 87-year-old woman whose eviction sparked housing ​protests in Spain, has died, Madrid's tenants' union said on Wednesday. Massive protests following Abascal's eviction spiralled into a political crisis, eventually leading Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez to call a snap election.</summary><category term="Europe" /></entry><entry><title>Trump says 'we don't think' Russian plague is bio-weapon</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b19444fef7a12a49f6d16c73e7ce77503990185997a3b68515951ef3dd88560a</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T19:04:48Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/trump-says-we-don-t-think-russian-plague-is-bio-weapon" /><summary type="html">US President Donald Trump said Wednesday that the death of a Russian plague laboratory worker did not appear to be linked to a biological weapon. "We don't think so. We're going to find out soon enough, but we don't think so," Trump told reporters in the Oval Office when asked if a "bio-weapon" was involved. Russia warned earlier against "false" information as reports emerged of a second suspected case. The US and UN have urged Russia to be more transparent. FRANCE 24's Fraser Jackson reports from Washington.</summary></entry><entry><title>'Resilience' of Israelis as they mourn the loss of loved ones and 'the sense that we can be safe'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:bc8df3ba408288cf6c5cffbdab26d6e5ae7734b8e6ac1d0c095a41f540e75ff0</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T17:46:29Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261007-resilience-of-israelis-as-they-the-mourn-loss-of-loved-ones-and-the-sense-that-we-can-be-safe" /><summary type="html">Angela Diffley is pleased to welcome Dr. Danny Brom, Founding Director of METIV, the Israel Psychotrauma Center in Jerusalem. According to Dr. Brom, as Israelis and Jewish people around the world continue to mourn the largest loss of Jewish life on a single day since the Holocaust, their fundamental sense of collective security has also been shattered. What strikes him, however, is the resilience that exists alongside the grieving. How can individuals, communities and society rebuild a sense of safety without allowing fear to dictate the future? Yet, as Dr. Brom observes, “the war is still going on,” while political leaders speak of pursuing “the ultimate victory, which no one really knows what that is.”</summary></entry><entry><title>France summons Iranian ambassador over 'disinformation' on student protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4835fab91f21c1eebe26079864fb4dec4f745571ddf9430f805dc2b174922ae7</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T17:23:52Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261007-france-summons-iranian-ambassador-over-disinformation-on-student-protests" /><summary type="html">The French foreign ministry on Wednesday summoned the Iranian ambassador to protest against a "disinformation campaign conducted by the Iranian authorities" over the student protests that have rocked France. The move came a day after Tehran summoned the French ambassador over the treatment of protesters in France.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>France suspends the use of stun grenades after student protester loses hand</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d9df9873c205b9987ee438d957813b1801c1ac5973248c1ceba42caf457e29bb</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T17:05:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261007-france-suspends-the-use-of-stun-grenades-after-student-protester-loses-hand" /><summary type="html">The French ​government said Wednesday it would suspend the use of stun grenades in its attempts to manage the student protests now roiling the country after a 15-year-old boy had his hand blown off during a clash with riot police.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>What France's student protesters want – and what the government is offering</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e2b4f949d6ebbd3883c270d298753a83952c2f42b461d69d0c58ab1e7d20e0fe</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T16:41:57Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261007-what-france-s-student-protesters-want-%E2%80%93-and-what-the-government-is-offering" /><summary type="html">Staff shortages, rats in the corridors, and a university admissions system that feels like a lottery: As protests sweep France, high school students have drawn up a list of demands to deal with these and other issues. Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu has promised a government response by the end of October, but whether he can quell the unrest remains to be seen.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>France: Government suspends stun grenade use in school protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9559007b041bd1bd6efc85dd9dc5e29d5c1c0bc6ddfd69a85471e907d78c786f</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T16:15:54Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261007-france-government-suspends-stun-grenade-use-in-school-protests" /><summary type="html">France has suspended police use of stun grenades at high school demonstrations, the interior minister announced, amid growing allegations of police brutality after two weeks of protests marked by frequent violent clashes that have left hundreds injured.France has suspended police use of stun grenades at high school demonstrations, the interior minister announced, amid growing allegations of police brutality after two weeks of protests marked by frequent violent clashes that have left hundreds injured.</summary></entry><entry><title>🔴 Live: France summons Iranian ambassador over 'disinformation' on student protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ba595ea8201ab28801f58014dbd6ba00f9ddc95cc842decbcade51205978bed6</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T15:52:25Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261007-middle-east-live-houthis-target-airport-in-yemen-as-israel-marks-october-7-anniversary" /></entry><entry><title>US woman who survived botched execution is conscious, speaking</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6ba1587860e87025740ca35317061988d0fc243e5b4a4ba193e18a91e81e369b</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T14:28:58Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261007-us-woman-who-survived-botched-execution-is-conscious-speaking" /><summary type="html">The woman who survived a failed execution in a US prison last week is now conscious and speaking, but her prognosis remains unclear, her lawyers said on Tuesday. Christa Pike, 50, who was sentenced to death for a murder she committed when she was a teenager, received two supposedly lethal doses of the drug pentobarbital at a Tennessee prison a week ago.</summary></entry><entry><title>Scars from Oct. 7 fuel deep divisions over Netanyahu ahead of Israeli election</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8c67dbf9f91846245e27d3147de8e53ef22e9d0a092446a3125ecd9c3c76cc15</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T14:23:22Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261007-scars-from-oct-7-fuel-deep-divisions-over-netanyahu-ahead-of-israeli-election" /><summary type="html">As Israel gears up for its first elections since Hamas' Oct. 7, 2023, attack, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and his opponents are putting it at the heart of their campaigns. Three years on, the worst security failure in Israel’s history now threatens to end the career of its longest-serving prime minister. FRANCE 24's Noga Tarnopolsky reports from Jerusalem.</summary></entry><entry><title>African tourists cancelling trip as xenophobia shadows South Africa's appeal</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5ff71df9a633d80411b748cbabae8ce4b8485e6b2ef78189a9c8e9f59c4e10c8</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T14:22:11Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261007-african-tourists-cancelling-trip-as-xenophobia-shadows-south-africa-s-appeal" /><summary type="html">South Africa has recorded record tourist arrivals over the past two months, with more than one million visitors arriving by air each month. But behind these encouraging figures, a concern is emerging: African tourists may gradually turn away from South Africa, even as tourism accounts for more than 8% of the country’s GDP. The country’s xenophobic image has left its mark, as FRANCE 24’s team on the ground reports.</summary></entry><entry><title>Brit adventurer back on UK soil after 28-year round-the-world walk</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3b899c6cf1d46e8a1cb558d2be4fe801bb3636decb784fa9b24f0ddb84feab10</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T14:19:06Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261007-brit-adventurer-back-on-uk-soil-after-28-year-round-the-world-walk" /><summary type="html">British adventurer on a 50,000-kilometre, 28-year trek home from South America without using any transport has set foot on UK soil after swimming the Channel, his publicists said Tuesday. Karl Bushby, a 57-year-old former paratrooper, set off from Punta Arenas, Chile, in November 1998, vowing to reach his mother's home near Hull in northern England without travelling on planes, trains, boats, vehicles or other transport modes.</summary></entry><entry><title>New Comoros law bans foreign nationals from certain jobs</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1d23f9808b27d05b1eaf942294c10faca66e7c3bf711ccffc6034de10d03144b</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T14:17:30Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261007-new-comoros-law-bans-foreign-nationals-from-certain-jobs" /><summary type="html">A new law in the Comoros bans foreign nationals from running certain small-scale businesses, threatening the livelihoods of thousands of people, most of them Malagasy. A measure that mirrors a growing wave of economic protectionism targeting migrant workers across the continent.</summary></entry><entry><title>A new 'Les Mis': This week in French film</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5aaadd332b7830ccd44d8b7d75f27ea551302f1e6d9e3541064b992bbebbd467</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T14:12:35Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/arts24/20261007-a-new-les-mis-this-week-in-french-film" /><summary type="html">This week on arts24, film critic Manon Kerjean takes us through some of the latest French cinema releases, starting with a new adaptation of Victor Hugo's "Les Misérables". Vincent Lindon plays Jean Valjean, opposite Tahar Rahim as Inspector Javert in a three-hour epic that brings a thriller-like tension to the classic story.</summary><category term="arts24" /></entry><entry><title>What we know about espionage, treason allegations against Germany's ex-spy chief</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:84c15e59aa756b87659fcff3c2c12e040aaf57a16a79c62f69b71338ecf686e3</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T14:06:59Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261007-what-we-know-about-espionage-treason-allegations-against-germany-s-ex-spy-chief" /><summary type="html">A court on Wednesday ordered the former head of Germany's foreign intelligence service August Hanning to remain in pretrial detention after his arrest on suspicion of espionage and treason, along with a former deputy who allegedly supplied him with secret files. Here's what we know about one of the biggest security breaches in German history.</summary><category term="Europe" /></entry><entry><title>From Mai 68 to 2026: France’s long student protests tradition</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6a763e8eab7b655829ae7604b37d98b165e1373dcbca1fc348cff9b0c18634c2</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T13:57:04Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261007-from-mai-68-to-2026-france-s-long-student-protests-tradition" /><summary type="html">Students, parents and trade unions were demonstrating across France Tuesday, protesting what they say is under-investment in high schools. From Mai 68 to the CPE crisis in 2006, major French student and youth protests have pressured leaders and transformed national politics.</summary></entry><entry><title>The secrets behind Mali junta’s takeover of Kidal without a fight</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e60957d36c60b952fc857c57747f525d77191c4bf3cb4910d0bc3d8acca35101</id><updated>2026-10-07T21:23:24Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T13:55:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261007-russia-backed-mali-junta-retakes-strategic-town-after-separatists-withdraw" /><summary type="html">The Malian army says it has entered the strategic northern city of Kidal, which was captured around five months ago by Tuareg separatists who announced their withdrawal earlier Tuesday as the military advanced – a symbolic boost to Mali's junta and its Russian backers. In late April, jihadists from Al-Qaeda-linked JNIM and its Tuareg separatist allies kicked off a series of attacks aimed at weakening Mali's junta rulers, including taking control of Kidal and killing the defence minister. FRANCE 24’s Senior journalist Wassim Nasr explains.</summary></entry></feed>
